🔍 Memory Areas in Java
Java memory is divided mainly into five memory areas managed by the JVM:

| # | Memory Area                       | Description                                                                                |
| - | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| 1 | **Heap**                          | Stores all **objects**, arrays, and instance variables. Shared across threads.             |
| 2 | **Stack**                         | Stores **method call frames**, **local variables**, and **references**. One per thread.    |
| 3 | **Method Area (MetaSpace)**       | Stores **class-level metadata** like class definitions, static variables, and method code. |
| 4 | **Program Counter Register (PC)** | Keeps track of the **next instruction address** to be executed in a thread.                |
| 5 | **Native Method Stack**           | Used when Java interacts with **native code** (e.g., C/C++ via JNI).                       |


🔎 1. Heap Memory
The largest memory area.

Used for:

All Java objects

Arrays

Class-level instance variables

🔸 Example:


String name = new String("Afham"); // Stored in heap
✅ Garbage Collected: JVM automatically removes unused objects from the heap.

🔎 2. Stack Memory
Each thread gets its own stack.

Stores:

Method calls

Local variables

Primitive values

References to objects (the objects themselves go to the heap)

🔸 Example:


int x = 10;        // primitive on stack
User user = new User(); // reference on stack, object in heap
⚠️ Stack memory is short-lived. When a method finishes execution, its stack frame is popped.

🔎 3. Method Area (MetaSpace in Java 8+)
Stores:

Class metadata (info about class, methods, fields)

Static variables

Method bytecode

🔸 Example:


public static String companyName = "Evamp"; // stored here
In Java 8+, this was moved to native memory and renamed MetaSpace (replacing PermGen).

🔎 4. Program Counter (PC) Register
A tiny memory area.

Each thread has its own PC.

It stores the address of the JVM instruction being executed.

Useful for JVM to switch between threads.

🔎 5. Native Method Stack
Supports native (non-Java) method execution, usually written in C or C++.

Uses Java Native Interface (JNI).

Not used unless your app calls native code.

📌 Summary Table
| Memory Area             | Stores                                      | Thread-safe  |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ------------ |
| **Heap**                | Objects, arrays, instance fields            | ✅ Shared     |
| **Stack**               | Method calls, local variables, references   | ❌ Per thread |
| **Method Area**         | Class info, static vars, method definitions | ✅ Shared     |
| **PC Register**         | Address of current JVM instruction          | ❌ Per thread |
| **Native Method Stack** | Native code execution support               | ❌ Per thread |


🔍 Example Java Code

public class MemoryExample {

    static String staticMessage = "I am static"; // stored in Method Area before java 8, and now

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 100;                          // stored in Stack
        String name = "Afham";                    // "Afham" is in String Pool (Heap), reference in Stack
        Person person = new Person("Ali", 25);    // Object in Heap, reference in Stack

        System.out.println(staticMessage);        // static variable from Method Area
        System.out.println(number);               // primitive from Stack
        System.out.println(name);                 // String from Heap via reference
        System.out.println(person.name);          // Object field from Heap
    }
}

class Person {
    String name;
    int age;

    Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;  // stored in Heap
        this.age = age;    // stored in Heap
    }
}




🧠 Clarification:
🔸 Before Java 8:
The Method Area was part of the Heap-like memory called PermGen (Permanent Generation).

Static variables, class metadata, and method bytecode were stored here.

🔸 Since Java 8:
PermGen was removed.

Replaced by MetaSpace, which is allocated in native memory, not the Java Heap.

So:

static fields

method code

class metadata
→ are still not stored in the Java Heap, but in MetaSpace.

🔍 What is Stored in the Heap?
Objects created via new

Instance variables (inside objects)

Arrays

Runtime string literals (outside the pool)



